.TH RMUT 1 "September 2026" "rmut 2.9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rmut \- a mutt-like terminal mail client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rmut
.RB [ \-R ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR CMD ]...
.RB [ \-p | \-y ]
.RB [ \-z | \-Z ]
.RI [ MAILDIR " | " MBOX " | " imap:ACCOUNT [/ FOLDER ] " | " mailto: URL ]
.br
.B rmut \-s
.I SUBJECT
.RB [ \-c
.IR CC ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR BCC ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR FILE ]...
.RB [ \-i
.IR FILE ]
.B \-\-
.IR ADDRESS ...
.br
.B rmut \-\-import\-muttrc
.RI [ MUTTRC ]
.br
.B rmut
.B \-V
|
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rmut
is a terminal mail client with mutt keybindings, built in Rust.
It reads maildirs, mbox files and IMAP folders, sends through
sendmail(1) or SMTP, threads messages, filters them with mutt-style
patterns, and signs/encrypts with gpg(1).
.PP
Without an argument it looks where mutt looks: the first mailbox from
.B mail.mailboxes
in the configuration, then
.BR mail.folder ,
.B $MAIL
(a maildir or an mbox file),
.IR ~/Maildir ", " ~/Mail ", " ~/mail ,
and the
.IR /var/mail/$USER " (or " /var/spool/mail/$USER ") spool."
A directory holding maildirs answers with its
.IR inbox ,
which is what a mutt-style
.I ~/Mail
usually is. Finding nothing is an error naming the places it tried
and how to make a maildir.
.SS The screen
Four regions, as in mutt: the help bar at the top, the mailbox or the
message, the status bar
.RB ( "[ui] status_format" ,
or the pager's
.BR "[pager] format" ),
and the message line under it. Prompts, notes and errors go on the
message line, so the status bar keeps saying which mailbox you are in
whatever else is happening.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-R
Open the mailbox read-only: nothing is ever written, not even read
marks; delete, flag, and sync refuse with a note, and the status
line's %r shows a %.
.TP
.BI \-f " MAILBOX"
The mailbox to open, the same as the positional argument.
.TP
.B \-p
Open the postponed picker straight away.
.TP
.B \-y
Open the mailbox list straight away.
.TP
.B \-z
Exit 1 instead of starting when the mailbox holds no messages.
.TP
.B \-Z
Exit 1 instead of starting when the mailbox holds no new mail.
Useful in a shell prompt or a cron job.
.TP
.BI \-e " COMMAND"
Run one enter-command line (see
.BR "ENTER-COMMAND" )
before the first draw, as
.B :
would. Repeatable, applied in order. In send mode only the
configuration half applies; bind, macro, push and exec need a running
interface and are ignored.
.TP
.BI \-s " SUBJECT"
Send without starting the interface. The body is read from standard
input (or
.BR \-i ),
the recipients are the remaining arguments, and the identity and
transport come from the configuration. The exit code says whether the
message went out.
.B \-c
and
.B \-b
add Cc and Bcc,
.B \-a
attaches a file and may be repeated, and
.B \-i
reads the body from a file instead of standard input. Any of these
flags, or a bare
.BR \-\- ,
puts rmut in send mode.
.TP
.BI mailto: URL
Open a draft prefilled from an RFC 6068 URL (to, cc, bcc, subject,
body), which is what a desktop mail handler is passed. The mailbox
still opens behind it, so the draft can be postponed as usual.
.TP
.BI \-\-import\-muttrc " \fR[\fPMUTTRC\fR]\fP"
Translate a muttrc (default
.I ~/.muttrc
or
.IR ~/.mutt/muttrc )
into rmut TOML on stdout, for review and saving as the configuration.
Directives with no rmut equivalent are kept as comments.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a short usage summary.
.SH MAILBOXES
.TP
.I MAILDIR
A maildir path. Flag changes and deletions sync on
.BR $ .
.TP
.I MBOX
An mbox file such as
.IR /var/mail/$USER .
The file is mirrored into a cache maildir;
.B $
rewrites the mbox in place under an exclusive flock (a backup copy is
kept in the cache during the rewrite) and refuses when the spool
changed since the mirror \(em refresh with
.B G
and sync again.
.TP
.IB imap: ACCOUNT [/ FOLDER ]
An IMAP folder of a configured
.B [[accounts]]
entry (INBOX when
.I FOLDER
is omitted), over TLS with local caching. A huge folder opens with
its newest 500 headers; the rest stream in through a background
connection while the index is already usable, the message count
climbing as they land.
.B $
maps flag changes and deletions back to the server. Dead connections
reconnect transparently; new mail arrives via IDLE or the regular
poll.
.SH KEYS
The bindings below are the defaults; the
.B [keys]
configuration remaps them, macros may shadow them, and
.B ?
always shows the live keymap.
An Alt key can be typed the way mutt spells it, as Esc and then the
key: in the index, the pager and the compose menu an Esc waits for the
next key and makes it the Alt binding however slowly it follows, so
Esc / is search-reverse like Alt+/. Esc twice is Esc's own binding.
.SS Index
.TP 12
.B j k Up Down
next / previous message
.TP
.B Space PgUp PgDn Ctrl+B Ctrl+F
page up / down
.TP
.B = *
first / last message (also Home / End)
.TP
.B Enter
view message
.TP
.B d u
mark for deletion / unmark
.TP
.B F
toggle flagged mark
.TP
.B N
toggle read/unread
.TP
.B t ;
toggle the tag; apply the next function to the tagged messages, with
.B Tag-
on the message line while it waits, as in mutt.
.B ;
reaches
.BR d ", " u ", " F ", " N ", " t ", " s ", " C ", " | ", " p " and " b :
the marks apply to each,
.BR s / C
save or copy them all under one prompt and one undo step,
.BR | / p
pipe or print them concatenated into a single run of the command
(mutt with $pipe_split unset), and
.B b
bounces them all to the same addresses. A function outside that set,
like resend, edit or the thread operations, says so rather than
quietly acting on the message under the cursor
.TP
.B z
cancel a message still held by
.BR undo_send ,
or else undo the last delete, undelete, flag, read toggle, tag,
pattern sweep, save or copy \(em one step per action, however many
messages it touched. The step remembers the messages as they stood, so undoing
puts their marks back, and a save's copy is removed again. Writing
the mailbox
.RB ( $ ", " q )
ends what can be undone: the stack is dropped, since those changes
are on disk. Undo is rmut's own; mutt has nothing like it
.TP
.B Tab Alt+Tab
jump to the next / previous new-or-unread message, wrapping around
(mutt's next-new-then-unread)
.TP
.B <digits> Enter
mutt's number entry: type a message's index, then Enter jumps to it
.TP
.B @
display\-address: the selected message's full From header on the
message line
.TP
.B %
toggle\-write: mark the mailbox read\-only or writable for the
session (mutt's toggle\-write; the \-R session flag cannot be undone
this way)
.TP
.B H M
move the cursor to the top / middle of the visible page (mutt's
top\-page / middle\-page; bottom\-page is an action with no default
key, since L is list\-reply, as in mutt)
.TP
.B D U T Ctrl+T
prompt for a pattern and delete / undelete / tag / untag every
matching message (within the active limit), reporting the count
.TP
.B $
write changes to the mailbox
.TP
.B q x
quit (asks before purging deletions) / quit without saving. Leaving
a mailbox ages unread new mail to old \(em shown as O, no longer
counted as new (mutt's mark_old).
.TP
.B m r g f
compose, reply, reply to all, forward
.TP
.B e Alt+e b
edit the raw message (mutt's edit; the changed result replaces the
original); edit as a new draft (resend); bounce to new recipients
.TP
.B o
choose sort order
.TP
.B l /  n
limit by pattern; search by pattern; repeat the last search the way
it was going.
.B Alt+/
searches backwards (mutt's search-reverse), and
.B n
then repeats backwards too. Both wrap around with a note, unless
.B "[mail] wrap_search = false"
(mutt's $wrap_search), which stops at the last or first match.
.TP
.B "l ~D" ", " "U " PATTERN
picking one message out of a run marked for deletion: deleted
messages stay in the index, so
.B j
and
.B k
land on them and
.B u
puts one back;
.B "l ~D"
limits the view to the marked ones, and
.BI "U " PATTERN
undeletes a whole set at once. A search hops between them
.RB ( /~D
then
.BR n ,
or Alt+/ the other way), which the macro example below binds to
.B .
and
.BR , .
.TP
.B c Alt+c y
open a mailbox by path; open one read-only (mutt's Esc c, where
.B \-R
is the whole session); browse nearby mailboxes. At the path prompt
Tab completes the configured mailboxes, the open account's folders
and nearby maildirs (repeated Tab cycles); Tab on an empty prompt
opens the folder browser. The save/copy prompts complete the same
way. Inside the browser
.B c
prompts for any directory to browse, Enter opens a maildir or
descends into a plain one (`..' goes up), and
.B C
creates a maildir (relative paths land in the browsed directory).
.TP
.B G
check for new mail now
.TP
.B v
list message parts (attachments); in that menu Enter views a text
part the way the pager would (a [filters] entry overrides any text,
mutt's auto_view; text/html goes through the built\-in renderer
unless [pager] html is \(dqraw\(dq) \(em any other type falls
through to its mailcap viewer, then to text, like mutt's
view\-attach,
.B m
views through mailcap regardless (view\-mailcap: a copiousoutput
viewer's text in the pager, an interactive viewer on the terminal,
the part decoded to a temp file that goes when the view ends),
.B T
shows the decoded bytes as text, raw source included (view\-text),
.B R
views through the built\-in html renderer by name, past any
[filters] entry and whatever [pager] html says \(em the way to
compare it against an auto_view filter, or to read a part whose
Content\-Type lies (not mutt's; added on request),
.B s
saves,
.B |
pipes the decoded part to a shell command,
.B p
prints it via print, and
.B f
forwards the part under the cursor: text is quoted as a forward
quotes a message, anything else goes along as a file, which
mime_forward_rest = false refuses instead (forward = "attach"
attaches it whatever it is)
.TP
.B Alt+v Alt+V
fold/unfold current thread; fold/unfold all threads
.TP
.B Alt+d Alt+u Alt+t
delete, undelete or tag the whole thread the cursor sits in (mutt's
Esc d / Esc u / Esc t). Tagging follows the cursor, so a second
Alt+t untags the thread; deleting then advances to the next
undeleted message (mutt's $resolve), while undeleting and tagging
stay put. Each is one undo step, however many messages hang off it
.TP
.B Ctrl+D Ctrl+U
the same, for the subthread: the message under the cursor and its
replies
.TP
.B Alt+a
mark every message in the mailbox read, one undo step (z takes it
back). Not mutt's; added on request
.TP
.B Alt+n Alt+p
jump to the next / previous thread, wrapping around with a note.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R Alt+r
mark the whole thread / the subthread read (mutt's Ctrl+R / Esc r);
one undo step
.TP
.B P
jump to the parent message (root\-message is an action too, unbound)
.TP
.B #
break\-thread: take the In\-Reply\-To and References off the message
under the cursor, so it and its replies become a thread of their own.
A message that was grouped by its subject alone (see
.B strict_threads
below) is marked with an
.B "X-Rmut-Thread: broken"
header instead, so the grouping leaves it alone from then on; mutt,
having nowhere to record that, hangs such a message straight back
.TP
.B &
link\-threads: make the tagged messages replies to the one under the
cursor, each given an In\-Reply\-To naming it and untagged, as
mutt's link_threads does. Both rewrite the message file in place, are
one undo step each (z writes the old headers back), and work on
local maildirs only: on IMAP and mbox the real message lives
elsewhere, so they refuse rather than edit a copy
.PP
All of these need thread sort
.RB ( "o t" ),
and say so when it is not on, as mutt does. tag\-subthread is an
action for bind, with no default key, as in mutt
.TP
.B s C
save (copy and mark deleted) / copy to a mailbox
.TP
.B | p
pipe raw message to a shell command; pipe to the print command
.TP
.B a
add the sender to the alias file
.TP
.B Q
look up addresses with query_command: the matches list in a menu,
Enter starts a message to the picked address
.TP
.B X
notmuch search: the query goes to
.B notmuch search \-\-output=files
and the hits open as a virtual read-only mailbox (symlinks into the
cache) \(em view, reply, copy, and pipe work there; flag changes
and deletes refuse, since the real copies live elsewhere. Keep the
database fresh with "notmuch new" in a delivery hook or cron;
disable the key with [mail] notmuch = false
.TP
.B B Ctrl+N Ctrl+P Ctrl+O
toggle the sidebar; move its highlight; open the highlighted mailbox
.TP
.B ! Ctrl+L Ctrl+Z
run a shell command with rmut stood down, waiting for Enter before
painting over what it printed; repaint the screen; suspend rmut, the
way any job suspends, with the terminal handed back and picked up
again on
.BR fg .
Ctrl+L works in every menu; Ctrl+Z in the index and the pager
.TP
.B ~
mark\-message: a stroke that becomes a hotkey jumping back to this
message (a search for its Message\-ID). mutt prefixes it with
$mark_macro_prefix; rmut's macros are one key, so the stroke is the
key.
.TP
.B Esc+L
list\-action: the message's RFC 2369 List\-* headers as a one\-key
menu (help, post, subscribe, unsubscribe, archives, owner). A
mailto: action opens a compose to it; anything else is refused, as
in mutt.
.TP
.B (unbound)
next\-unread\-mailbox opens the next configured mailbox holding new
mail; purge\-message deletes past the trash; error\-history shows the
recent errors ($error_history of them); what\-key names every key
until Ctrl+G. Bind them by name, or run them with :exec.
.B urls
lists the selected message's links, as it does in the pager (Ctrl+B
there); in the index it has no key, Ctrl+B being page\-up.
.SS Pager
The URLs in the pager are OSC 8 hyperlinks: a terminal that knows
them (kitty, foot, WezTerm, iTerm2, GNOME Terminal and other VTE
ones, Windows Terminal) opens one on a click, a URL the pager wrapped
opening whole from either row. [ui] hyperlinks = false turns them off
for a terminal that shows the sequence instead.
.B Ctrl+B
(urls) lists the message's links, headers first, each once:
.B Enter
opens the one under the cursor with [ui] url_command,
.B y
copies it to the clipboard through OSC 52 (over ssh too, with
nothing installed), and
.B q
goes back. Neither is mutt's; they are urlview built in.
.PP
The body is the whole MIME tree rendered like mutt: text parts and
[filters]-rendered parts inline, each attachment announced with
.B [\-\- Attachment #N \-\-]
marker lines, multipart/alternative collapsed to its best part
(alternative_order first, then auto_view filters, then text/plain
over text/html), embedded
message/rfc822 shown as its headers plus body, and a one\-line stub
for anything undisplayable. One deliberate step past mutt: a
text/html part that no [filters] or mailcap auto_view entry claims
renders through a built\-in html\-to\-text (entities decoded, block
elements breaking lines, links keeping their targets as
.IR "text <url>" ,
blockquotes becoming
.B >
prefixes so the quote colours and
.B T
work on html mail); set
.B "[pager] html"
to
.B \(dqraw\(dq
for mutt's literal source view.
.TP 12
.B q i\fR, \fBEsc Esc
back to the index
.TP
.B Enter Backspace
scroll one line
.TP
.B d u F t
delete and advance; undelete; toggle the flagged mark; toggle the
tag, all without leaving the message. Each is one undo step, like its
index twin. toggle-new has no key here, since
.B N
is the backwards text search; bind it with
.RB \(dq ":bind pager KEY toggle-new" \(dq
if you want it
.TP
.B j k Down Up Right Left
open the next / previous undeleted message (mutt's pager default;
in an attachment part view these refuse with "Not available in this
menu.")
.TP
.B Space \-
page down / up (also PgDn / PgUp, Home / End for top / bottom);
Space past the end opens the next undeleted message (mutt's
pager_stop default) \(em except when viewing a single attachment
part, which
returns to the attachment menu, as does q; wrapped lines carry a
leading + (mutt's markers)
.TP
.B Ctrl+D Ctrl+U
scroll half a page down / up
.TP
.B T S
show/hide quoted text; skip past the quoted text below. Quoted
lines are tinted by depth (see quote_regexp and the quoted colors);
search hits are highlighted
.TP
.B J K
open next / previous message (mutt's next-entry: deleted ones too)
.TP
.B d
delete and advance to the next undeleted
.TP
.B h
toggle full headers
.TP
.B / n N
search the displayed text (case-insensitive, regex like the
patterns; unlike the index /, which matches whole messages);
.B n
repeats forward,
.B N
backwards, both wrap around with a note.
.B \e
(search\-toggle) hides and restores the hit highlighting without
forgetting the pattern. Moving to another message ends the search,
like mutt: the next n re-prompts, prefilled with the last pattern
.PP
.BR v ", " m ", " r ", " g ", " f ", " p ", " s ", " C ", " | ,
.BR b ", " e " and " a
work as in the index.
.SS While the network is busy
The IMAP connection runs on a thread of its own, so the screen keeps
drawing and the keys keep working while it talks. The message line
says what it is doing ("fetching the message...", "syncing..."), and
.B Ctrl+G
gives up on it, as it does in mutt: the socket goes down, the
operation says it was aborted, and the next one reconnects. The poll
tick, a sync, and fetching a message body all work this way; opening
a mailbox and the folder browser's listing still wait, bounded by
.B [net] connect_timeout .
.SS Prompts
Every line prompt is a small line editor: Left/Right, Home/End (also
ctrl+a / ctrl+e), Backspace and Del (ctrl+d) around the cursor,
ctrl+u kills to the start, ctrl+k to the end, ctrl+w the word before
the cursor. Up/Down recall the prompt kind's history (patterns,
addresses, mailboxes, files, commands), newest first. The history is
per session unless
.B "[ui] history_file"
(mutt's $history_file) names a file to persist it in, up to
.B save_history
entries a kind (default 100).
.SS Compose questions
Like mutt's ask-yes defaults: replying to a message with a Reply-To
asks "Reply to ...?" (no falls back to From), an empty Subject asks
"No subject, abort?", and replies ask "Include message in reply?"
before quoting the original. Enter takes the default (yes).
.SS Compose menu
After the editor, the draft lands in mutt's compose menu: the header
block (From/To/Cc/Bcc/Subject/Fcc/Security) above the attachment
table (the body, a forwarded original, every Attach: file with size
and type).
.B y
sends,
.B e
reopens the editor,
.B Enter
views the selected entry (text directly, other types through their
[filters] command),
.BR t / c / b / s
edit To/Cc/Bcc/Subject (address prompts Tab-complete),
.BR F / r
edit the From and Reply-To,
.B a
attaches a file,
.B A
attaches the tagged messages of the open mailbox (the one under the
cursor when none is tagged) as message/rfc822 parts,
.B n
makes a new file of a given Content\-Type, attaches it and opens it
in the editor (mutt's new\-mime),
.B Ctrl+O
renames the selected file for sending (mutt's rename\-attachment;
"@name=" on its Attach: line),
.B u
marks it to be unlinked once the message is sent ("@unlink"),
.B Ctrl+D
toggles its disposition between attachment and inline ("@inline"),
.BR K / J
move it up and down the table,
.B w
writes the message as it stands to a mailbox without sending it
(mutt's write\-fcc),
.B i
runs $ispell over the draft ("ispell \-x FILE"),
.B V
views the selected file through its mailcap entry and
.B Esc+v
as text whatever its type,
.B D
detaches the selected file
.RB ( j / k
move),
.B d
edits the selected attachment's description and
.B Ctrl+T
its content-type (both stored on the Attach: line as
"Attach: path [type/subtype] [description]"),
.B f
edits the Fcc (empty keeps no sent copy),
.B p
opens the PGP menu (encrypt/sign/both/clear),
.B P
postpones, and
.B q
asks "Postpone this message?" \(em no discards. mutt's $postpone
sets that question (yes / no skip it, ask\-yes / ask\-no choose the
default), and $recall the new\-or\-recall offer when drafts wait
(no never asks, yes recalls the newest, the default asks). Recalling
with several postponed drafts offers a picker.
.PP
Related [mail] switches: fast_reply skips the To/Subject prompts on
replies (Subject on forwards); autoedit (with edit_headers) skips
every prompt and question, straight into the editor;
forward = "ask" asks "Forward as attachment?" per forward;
copy = false keeps no sent copy by default; askcc and askbcc add
those prompts between To and Subject, as mutt's do.
.PP
The send-side odds, all mutt's: use_envelope_from hands sendmail
.B \-f
with envelope_from_address, or the From when that is unset (over
SMTP it replaces the From in MAIL FROM); dsn_notify (e.g.
"failure,delay") and dsn_return ("hdrs" or "full") become sendmail's
.BR \-N " and " \-R ,
and NOTIFY= and RET= to an SMTP server that offers DSN. A reply to
your own message goes to its recipients unless reply_self is set.
fcc_attach (a quadoption, yes by default) keeps the attachments in
the sent copy or, at no, the text alone; fcc_clear keeps the copy of
a signed or encrypted message in the clear. forward_edit (yes by
default) set to no takes a forward straight to the compose menu, and
ask\-yes / ask\-no ask "Edit forwarded message?" first.
.PP
The quoted reply and the forward subject are format strings over the
message being answered:
.B attribution
(default "On %d, %n wrote:"),
.B indent_string
(default "> ") and
.B forward_format
(default "[%a: %s]"), with %a the author's address, %n their name, %f
the From header as written, %s the subject, %i the message-id, %d the
date and %{...} the date through strftime.
.B include
decides whether a reply quotes at all: yes, no, ask\-yes (the
default) or ask\-no, the last two asking and taking their own answer
on Enter.
.B forward_quote
brings the forwarded message in quoted with indent_string too; the
"----- Forwarded message" markers stay flush either way.
.B reply_regexp
(mutt's, default "^(re)(\\[[0-9]+\\])*:[ \\t]*") is what a subject may
already start with: a reply takes that off and puts "Re: " on, so
prefixes never pile up. Case\-insensitive unless the regex holds an
uppercase letter, as mutt compiles it.
.PP
.B signature
names a file whose contents end every draft rmut starts, or, when it
ends in a pipe (|), a command whose output does; it is read afresh
each time, and one that cannot be read is left off.
.B sig_dashes
(on by default, as in mutt) puts a line holding "\-\- " above it.
.PP
.B abort_nosubject
is the empty-subject question: ask\-yes (the default) asks with Enter
aborting, ask\-no asks with Enter keeping the draft, yes aborts
without asking and no never asks.
.B abort_unmodified
(on by default, as in mutt) drops a draft whose first editor pass
changed nothing, which is what quitting the editor looks like from
here; a re\-edit from the compose menu is never dropped.
.SH ENTER\-COMMAND
.B :
opens a command prompt (the line editor, with its own history) and
applies one configuration line to the running session. Nothing is
written back to the configuration file.
.PP
.nf
  :set index_format="%4C %Z %{%b %d} %-15.15L %s"
  :set nobeep          # also set beep, unset beep, toggle beep
  :set invtilde        # mutt's inv prefix toggles
  :set sort?           # report a value instead of setting it
  :bind index \\Cd delete-message
  :macro pager S "s=archive<enter>"
  :reset pager_context     # back to the default; unset goes to nothing
  :color index brightyellow default ~F
  :uncolor index ~F        # or uncolor index *, uncolor status, ...
  :mono index bold ~N      # an attribute for a colour pair; unmono undoes
  :unhook folder-hook      # drop a hook type, or unhook *
  :unmailboxes ~/Mail/old  # stop watching one, or unmailboxes *
  :unalias bob             # out of the alias file, or unalias *
  :ignore x-spam-score     # and unignore, to bring one back
  :alternates 'jane@old\.example\.com'  # unalternates removes
  :my_hdr Organization: Acme   # unmy_hdr Organization removes
  :auto_view text/html         # unauto_view text/html removes
  :alternative_order text/plain text/html   # unalternative_order
  :alias jane Jane Doe <jane@example.com>
  :push "<enter>"      # keys into the input queue
  :exec sync           # run one function now
.fi
.PP
Settable at runtime: index_format, date_format, sort, sort_aux,
pager_format, pager_index_lines, pager_context, quote_regexp, wrap,
tilde, status_format, theme, beep, from, realname, reverse_name,
edit_headers, fast_reply, autoedit, copy, forward, mime_forward,
attribution, indent_string, forward_format, include, askcc, askbcc,
connect_timeout, net_timeout, pager_stop, markers, smart_wrap,
collapse_unread, uncollapse_jump, quit, confirmappend, save_name,
force_name, forward_quote, signature, sig_dashes, abort_nosubject,
abort_unmodified, mark_old, print, beep_new, wait_key,
reverse_realname, delete_untag, flag_safe, maildir_trash,
mail_check_recent, check_new, uncollapse_new, menu_scroll,
menu_context, menu_move_off, help, error_history, sort_browser,
sort_alias, shell, tmpdir, ispell,
sendmail, editor, print_command, query_command, trash, record,
postponed, new_mail_command, mail_check, undo_send, metoo, text_flowed,
reflow_text, notmuch, sidebar_visible,
sidebar_width, pgp_sign_as, crypt_autosign, crypt_autoencrypt,
use_envelope_from (and its old name envelope_from),
envelope_from_address, dsn_notify, dsn_return, reply_self,
fcc_attach, fcc_clear, forward_edit, mime_forward_rest, hyperlinks,
url_command.
An unknown option, a bad number, an unbindable key or an unknown
function reports on the bottom line in the error colour and stops the
rest of the line. Changed settings recompile in place: colours, key
tables, the quote regexp, header rules, and a resort when the sort
order moved.
.SH PATTERNS
Limit and search take mutt-style patterns. On an IMAP mailbox a
plain-substring ~b term is answered by the server (UID SEARCH), so
no bodies are downloaded; regex or non-ASCII terms, and any failure,
fall back to matching locally. The specifiers:
.PP
.nf
  ~f x  from       ~s x  subject     ~b x  body
  ~t x  to         ~c x  cc          ~C x  to or cc
  ~e x  sender     ~h x  any header  ~i x  Message-ID
  ~x x  References ~d spec  date     ~r spec  received date
  ~m spec  index range        ~z spec  size range
  ~=  duplicate Message-ID    ~l  to a known mailing list
  ~u  to a subscribed list    ~y x  X-Label
  ~L x  from, to or cc        ~B x  headers and body
  ~O old   ~R read   ~Q replied
  ~(P) some message in the thread matches P
  ~<(P) the parent matches P  ~>(P) a child matches P
  ~v  head of a folded thread ~$  no parent and no children
  word  a bare word expands via simple_search (from/subject)
  ~N new   ~U unread   ~F flagged   ~D deleted   ~T tagged
  ~p addressed to me         ~P sent by me
  ~A every message
.fi
.PP
.I x
is a case-insensitive regex; "quotes" keep spaces. A bare single
word with no ~ expands through
.B "[mail] simple_search"
(mutt's, default "~f %s | ~s %s"): set it to "~f %s | ~s %s | ~b %s"
to search the body too. Date specs:
.BR 24/12/2026 ,
.BR 1/6/2026\-30/6/2026 ,
.BR 24/12\- ,
.BR <1w ", " >2d ", " =3d .
.B ~r
takes the same specs against the file's delivery time.
.B ~m
counts the index as shown
.RB ( "~m 10\-20" ", " "~m 5\-" ", " "~m \-20" ),
with
.B .
for the selected message and
.B $
for the last
.RB ( "~m .\-$" ).
.B ~z
takes
.BR >100K ", " <2M ", " 1K\-2M ,
or a plain byte count; K, M and G are powers of 1024.
The thread terms want thread sort; outside it ~(P) reads as P and
the other four are false.
Terms AND;
.B !
negates,
.B |
ORs,
.B ()
groups:
.B !~D (~f jane | ~t jane) ~d <1m
.SS Labels and flags
.B Y
(edit\-label) sets, changes or clears the X\-Label of the message
under the cursor, or the tagged set with the tag prefix; an empty
value clears it. It rewrites the message file in place (local
maildirs only, like the thread edits) and is one undo step.
.B ~y
matches it,
.B %y
shows it, and
.B "o y"
sorts by it, unlabelled last.
.B V
prints the rmut version and
.B Alt+l
the active limit pattern.
The pattern terms mutt lists but rmut does not carry (the crypto
~g ~G ~V ~k, the attachment count ~X, ~S/~E, and mutt's scoring
~n ~H) say so by name rather than reading as unknown.
.SH CONFIGURATION
TOML, read from
.B $RMUT_CONFIG
or
.IR ~/.config/rmut/config.toml .
A missing file means defaults; a broken file is ignored with a
warning. All sections and keys are optional.
.SS [identity]
.TP
.B name, email
The From identity.
.TP
.B reverse_name
A reply's From becomes whichever of your addresses the original was
sent to (mutt's reverse_name).
.SS [[folder_hooks]], [[message_hooks]], [[reply_hooks]]
mutt's folder-hook, message-hook and reply-hook, each carrying one
enter-command line in
.BR command .
A folder-hook takes
.B folder
(a glob on the opened mailbox) and runs when that mailbox opens, at
startup as well as on a switch; like mutt it is not undone on the way
out, so a catch-all entry is how a setting goes back. A message-hook
takes
.B pattern
and is in force only while a matching message is the selected one:
when the match set changes, everything it touched goes back. A
reply-hook applies while a reply to a matching message is built,
which covers
.BR "set from" ", " edit_headers " and " my_hdr .
.SS [[fcc_hooks]]
mutt's fcc-hook:
.B pattern
is matched against the draft as it stands after the editor and
.B mailbox
is the local maildir its copy goes to. The compose menu's Fcc line
shows the hook's answer, an Fcc chosen by hand with
.B f
still wins, and batch sends honour it too. Bcc addresses join the Cc
ones for matching, so
.B ~c
sees a blind recipient.
.SS [[crypt_hooks]]
mutt's crypt-hook:
.B address
is a case-insensitive regex over a recipient address and
.B key
the key id gpg is asked to encrypt to instead.
.SS [[identities]]
Conditional identities, applied in order over
.B [identity]
and the account identity \(em the minimal folder-/send-hook. Keys:
.B folder
(glob on the open mailbox, path or imap: spec),
.B recipient
(glob on any draft recipient),
.BR name ", " email .
With both globs set both must match; unset name/email keep the value
from the layer below.
.SS [mail]
.TP
.B mailboxes
Mailboxes for the folder browser, new-mail watching and the sidebar;
the first is the default mailbox. In the browser (\fBy\fR),
.B C
creates a folder (an imap:account/name spec makes a remote one),
.B d
deletes the selected mailbox (confirmed),
.B r
renames it, and
.B s / u
subscribe and unsubscribe: CREATE / DELETE / RENAME / SUBSCRIBE on
the open IMAP account, or the filesystem for a local maildir.
.TP
.B sent, postponed
Mailboxes receiving sent copies and postponed drafts.
.TP
.B sendmail
The delivery command (default
.IR /usr/sbin/sendmail );
SMTP is used instead for imap: mailboxes with an smtp_host.
.TP
.B editor
Draft editor; default $VISUAL, $EDITOR, then vi.
.TP
.B poll_seconds
New-mail poll interval.
.TP
.B print
Command the printed message is piped to (default lpr).
.TP
.B pipe_decode ", " print_decode
Pipe / print the decoded message (weeded headers, decoded body)
rather than the raw one. pipe_decode is off, print_decode on, as in
mutt. The
.B "Alt+s"
(decode\-save) and
.B "Alt+C"
(decode\-copy) keys deliver that decoded form to a mailbox whatever
these say.
.TP
.B pipe_split ", " print_split
Run the command once per tagged message instead of once over them
all. Off by default.
.TP
.B pipe_sep
What separates concatenated messages in one pipe run (default
newline).
.TP
.B save
Default target offered by
.BR s .
.TP
.B forward
"inline" (quoted text, the default) or "attach" (the original goes
along as a message/rfc822 part, mutt's mime_forward).
.TP
.B query_command
External address lookup for Tab completion at the To prompt (%s is
the word, or it is appended), e.g. "khard email \-\-parsable %s".
.TP
.B trash
Purged messages move here (a maildir path or imap:account/folder of
the open account) instead of being erased; purging inside the trash
deletes for real (mutt's $trash).
.TP
.B edit_headers
mutt's edit_headers (default false, like mutt): true puts the
draft's header block (From/To/Cc/Subject, Attach: lines) into the
editor buffer for direct editing. Off, the prompts set To and
Subject, and attachments go through the compose menu's
.BR a .
.TP
.B new_mail_command
Shell command run (fire-and-forget) when new mail arrives, in the
open mailbox or a watched one: %f is the mailbox, %n how many.
E.g. "notify-send 'rmut: %n new in %f'".
.TP
.B lists, subscribed
Case-insensitive address regexes naming the mailing lists you know of
and the ones you are on (mutt's lists and subscribe).
.B subscribed
counts as a known list too. They drive
.BR ~l ,
the
.B L
list-reply target, and the Mail-Followup-To on mail to a list.
.TP
.B alternates
Case-insensitive regexes for your other addresses (mutt's
alternates), joined to the identity addresses everywhere rmut asks
whether an address is yours:
.BR ~p " and " ~P ,
the
.B %Z
mark, reverse_name, the group-reply dedup, and Mail-Followup-To.
.TP
.B my_hdr
Header lines added to every draft, e.g. "Organization: Acme" or
"Bcc: me@example.com" (mutt's my_hdr). One naming a header rmut
already wrote replaces it, so From: and Reply-To: win; To, Cc and Bcc
gain the address instead. One entry per header name.
.TP
.B metoo
Keep your own address among a group reply's recipients (mutt's
$metoo); off by default, so replying to all does not mail you a copy.
.TP
.B text_flowed
Declare the outgoing text part
.I text/plain; format=flowed
and space-stuff it (RFC 3676, mutt's $text_flowed), in the plain case,
under attachments, and inside a PGP layer alike. The paragraphs are
your editor's doing: a line that continues has to end with a space,
and rmut adds none of its own.
.TP
.B delete
mutt's $delete quadoption: what
.B $
and quitting do with messages marked for deletion.
.B ask
(the default) asks, and like mutt's ask-yes the Enter takes the yes;
.B yes
purges without asking;
.B no
never purges, so the marks keep until you change your mind. mutt's
ask-yes and ask-no both import as ask.
.TP
.B abort_noattach ", " attach_keyword
neomutt's $abort_noattach and $abort_noattach_regex: when the body
mentions an attachment and none is attached,
.B ask
questions the send and
.B yes
refuses it;
.B no
(the default) never looks. Answering no returns to the compose menu,
where
.B a
attaches the file. Quoted lines and anything below a
.B "-- "
signature do not count, so a reply to someone else's "see attached"
is not a false alarm. neomutt's ask-yes and ask-no both import as
ask, and mutt's
.BR \e< " / " \e>
word edges are translated to
.BR \eb .
Batch sends never ask, having no terminal to answer at.
.TP
.B undo_send
Seconds a sent message waits before anything leaves the machine, so
.B z
can take it back; 0 (the default) sends at once, as mutt does. The
status line counts down, and cancelling returns the message to its
compose menu with the draft as it was. The timer running out sends
it, and so does leaving rmut: quitting is not cancelling. Batch sends
never hold, having no terminal to press
.B z
at. rmut's own; mutt has no equivalent.
.TP
.B folder
mutt's $folder: where mailboxes live, so
.BI = x
and
.BI + x
name a mailbox under it and
.BR = " or " +
alone is the folder itself. Expanded wherever a mailbox is named: the
change-folder, save and copy prompts, the compose menu's Fcc, and the
configured mailboxes, trash, sent, postponed and fcc-hook targets, as
well as inside a macro, which is what lets an imported
.RB \(dq "<save-message>=archive<enter>" \(dq
land where it means to. An account spec works as the folder
("imap:work"), making
.B =Archive
that account's Archive. Tab completion works on real paths only.
.SS [index]
.TP
.B format
The index line, see
.B FORMAT STRINGS
below.
.TP
.B sort
Initial sort: date, from, to, subject, size, threads, label or
unsorted (mutt's mailbox\-order), with an optional "reverse\-"
prefix.
.TP
.B sort_aux
Which thread comes first, in mutt's spellings: a bare date orders each
thread by its root,
.B last\-
by its newest message, and a
.B reverse\-
prefix turns the threads round while leaving each thread's own order
alone ("reverse\-last\-date\-received" is the usual newest\-first).
.TP
.B date_format
chrono strftime string for the date column (default "%b %e", like
mutt's index date).
.SS [pager]
.TP
.B index_lines
Lines of the message index kept visible above the pager.
.TP
.B context
Lines of overlap when paging (mutt's pager_context).
.TP
.B search_context
Lines of context kept above a pager search hit scrolled toward the
top (mutt's $search_context, default 0).
.TP
.B quote_regexp
Regex classifying quoted body lines (mutt's $quote_regexp; default
"^([ \\t]*[|>:}#])+"). The depth \(em the number of quote characters
in the match \(em picks the quoted color.
.TP
.B ignore unignore hdr_order
mutt's header weeding for the brief view: lists of header-name
prefixes ("*" matches all); ignored headers are hidden unless a
prefix in unignore re-includes them, and hdr_order sorts the shown
ones (unlisted names follow in message order). Unset, the classic
view shows Date/From/To/Cc/Subject. The h key still toggles the
full list.
.TP
.B format
mutt's $pager_format for the pager's bottom line: %C message
number, %m count, %n sender, %s subject, %Z status characters,
%P percent through the message, %f mailbox, plus the conditional
and %>X machinery. Default: "\-\-\-Message %C/%m: %s \-\- %P".
.TP
.B wrap
mutt's $wrap: wrap body text at N columns instead of the window
width; a negative N leaves a right margin of |N|.
.TP
.B tilde
mutt's $tilde: pad the rows below end-of-message with ~.
.TP
.B html
.B \(dqraw\(dq
shows a text/html part as literal source, as mutt does; unset (or
anything else) renders it through the built\-in html\-to\-text
described under
.BR Pager .
A [filters] or mailcap auto_view entry for text/html always wins,
since it is consulted first. The window's Preferences dialog can
override this for the window alone ([gui] html)
.TP
.B reflow_text
mutt's $reflow_text (on by default): a
.I format=flowed
part is put back into paragraphs and wrapped at the display width,
rather than kept on the sender's line breaks. Quote depth bounds a
paragraph and survives as
.B >
marks, space-stuffing is undone, DelSp is honoured, and
.B "-- "
stays a fixed line.
.TP
.B alternative_order
mutt's alternative_order: MIME types, most wanted first, deciding
which part of a multipart/alternative shows.
.BR text/* -style
wildcards match a whole main type. Consulted before the auto_view
filters and the built-in text ranking; a message with none of the
listed types falls back to them.
.SS [net]
.TP
.B connect_timeout
Seconds to wait for a connection, default 10; 0 waits as long as the
OS does, which is about two minutes with nothing on screen. mutt's
$connect_timeout, and
.B \-\-import\-muttrc
brings it over.
.TP
.B timeout
Seconds to wait for data on a live connection, default 30. Never off,
and never under five: IMAP IDLE ticks on it to notice a stop request,
and re-issues itself about every 25 minutes whatever it is set to.
A timeout says which host and what it was doing.
.TP
.B system_cas
Trust the operating system's certificate store on top of the built\-in
Mozilla roots (mutt's $ssl_usesystemcerts). On by default. The trust
settings only ever add anchors to the Mozilla baseline; they cannot
take the default roots away.
.TP
.B certificate_file
A PEM file of extra roots to trust \(em a private CA, or a self\-signed
server's own certificate (mutt's $certificate_file /
$ssl_ca_certificates_file). Added to the roots, never replacing them;
rmut has no interactive accept\-once, no client\-certificate auth and
no $tunnel yet, so those import as skipped.
.SS [mail], the leaving and filing habits
.TP
.B quit
"yes" (the default) leaves at once, "no" refuses,
"ask\-yes" and "ask\-no" ask first (mutt's $quit).
.TP
.B confirmappend
Ask before adding messages to a mailbox that already exists (mutt's
$confirmappend). Off by default, where mutt asks.
.TP
.B save_name, force_name
The save prompt offers the mailbox named after the sender's local
part, under
.BR folder :
save_name when it exists, force_name whether or not (mutt's
$save_name and $force_name).
.TP
.B signature ", " sig_dashes
mutt's $signature: a file whose contents end every draft rmut starts
(a reply under the quoted original), or a command when the name ends
in a pipe (|). It is read for each draft, so a generated one can say
something new; unreadable is no signature at all. sig_dashes, on
unless turned off, puts a line holding "\-\- " above it, which is also
what the attachment reminder treats as the end of the message.
.B sig_on_top
(mutt's, off by default) puts the signature above the quoted original
instead of below.
.TP
.B hostname ", " user_agent
mutt's $hostname sets the host in a generated Message\-ID (the system
name when unset). mutt's $user_agent, off by default, adds a
.I "User-Agent: rmut/VERSION"
header to outgoing mail.
.TP
.B forward_quote
mutt's $forward_quote: the forwarded message comes in quoted with
indent_string, so a reply to the forward reads right. Off by default,
as in mutt; the markers around it are never quoted.
.TP
.B abort_nosubject
mutt's $abort_nosubject: what an empty Subject does. ask\-yes (the
default) asks with Enter aborting, ask\-no asks with Enter keeping
the draft, yes aborts without asking, no never asks.
.TP
.B abort_unmodified
mutt's $abort_unmodified: the first editor pass came back with the
draft untouched, so there is no message and it is dropped (on by
default, as in mutt). Only the first pass counts: a re\-edit from the
compose menu can change nothing without losing the draft.
.TP
.B mark_old
mutt's $mark_old: unread mail you leave behind ages to old when you
leave the mailbox \(em shown as O, out of the new count (on by
default, as in mutt). Off, it is still new next time.
.TP
.B delete_untag
mutt's $delete_untag: marking a tagged message for deletion, or
saving it away, takes its tag off (on by default, as in mutt).
.TP
.B flag_safe
mutt's $flag_safe: a flagged message cannot be marked for deletion,
by any of the ways of doing so (off by default).
.TP
.B maildir_trash
mutt's $maildir_trash: a purge gives deleted messages the maildir T
flag instead of unlinking them; they stay in the index marked D.
Maildir only (off by default).
.TP
.B mail_check_recent
mutt's $mail_check_recent: "new mail in X" only when X has grown
since the last look (on by default). Off announces any mailbox
holding new mail, once, until it empties.
.TP
.B check_new
mutt's $check_new: look for mail delivered to the open maildir while
it is open (on by default). Off stops the rescan; IMAP is unaffected.
.TP
.B sort_alias
mutt's $sort_alias: the order address completion offers alias
expansions in \(em address (by the expansion, the default) or alias
(by nick; unsorted reads the same, the file being a map). A reverse\-
prefix flips it.
.TP
.B shell
mutt's $shell: what a bare ! (an empty shell command) runs
interactively. $SHELL, then sh, when unset. Anything typed runs
under sh \-c, as mutt's does.
.TP
.B tmpdir
mutt's $tmpdir: where temporary files go (a draft on its way to the
editor, a part on its way to a viewer). $TMPDIR, then /tmp, when
unset.
.TP
.B ispell
mutt's $ispell: the spell checker the compose menu's i runs over the
draft, as "ispell \-x FILE". ispell when unset.
.TP
.B print_confirm
mutt's $print, the question before
.B p
prints: ask\-no (the default, and mutt's) asks with Enter declining,
ask\-yes asks with Enter printing, yes prints without asking and no
refuses to print at all. The command itself is
.BR print .
.TP
.B beep_new ", " wait_key
[ui] settings, both mutt's. beep_new rings the terminal bell when
mail arrives, as $beep rings it for an error (off by default);
wait_key ends a shell escape with "Press Enter to continue", so what
the command printed can be read before the index paints over it (on
by default). Turning wait_key off brings the screen straight back.
.TP
.B reverse_realname
mutt's $reverse_realname, an [identity] setting that only matters
with reverse_name: on (the default) a reply's From is the address
\fIand\fP the display name the message was addressed under; off,
only the address comes over and the configured name stays.
.SS [index] and [pager], the reading habits
.TP
.B pager_stop
Space on the last page of a message stays there instead of opening the
next one (mutt's $pager_stop, off by default).
.TP
.B markers
The
.B +
at the start of a wrapped continuation line (mutt's $markers, on).
.TP
.B smart_wrap
Wrapped lines break at a word boundary rather than at the column
(mutt's $smart_wrap, on).
.TP
.B collapse_unread
Off leaves a thread holding unread mail open when everything else
folds (mutt's $collapse_unread, on).
.TP
.B uncollapse_jump
Unfolding a thread puts the cursor on its first unread message
(mutt's $uncollapse_jump, off).
.TP
.B hide_thread_subject
A thread reply whose subject repeats its parent's shows only the tree
arrow (mutt's $hide_thread_subject). Off by default here, where mutt
has it on, so rmut's look is unchanged until asked.
.TP
.B uncollapse_new
A folded thread that receives a new message unfolds (mutt's
$uncollapse_new, on). Off, it stays folded and the count grows.
.TP
.B strict_threads
Thread by In\-Reply\-To and References only (mutt's $strict_threads,
off). Left off, as in mutt, a thread root whose subject repeats one
already in the mailbox is hung under the message that named it, which
is what threads mail sent with no threading headers at all: a
notification robot, or a list that strips them. Where the subject,
not a reference, decided the place, the tree ends in a star instead of
an arrow, as mutt's does.
.TP
.B sort_re
Only a root whose subject carries the $reply_regexp prefix ("Re:") is
grouped that way (mutt's $sort_re, on). Off, any equal subject is
grouped, unrelated mail sharing a subject like "hi" included.
.SS [ui]
.TP
.B theme
"default" or "mono".
.TP
.B status_format
The bottom status line, see
.B FORMAT STRINGS
below.
.TP
.B hyperlinks
The pager's URLs as OSC 8 hyperlinks (default true).
.TP
.B url_command
What opens a URL from the link list and the window's pager, the URL
its last argument or in place of %s (default xdg\-open, open on
macOS). Run without a shell.
.TP
.B beep
Ring the terminal bell on error statuses (mutt's $beep, default
true). Errors also take over the bottom line in the error color.
.TP
.B set_title, title_format
mutt's $ts_enabled / $ts_status_format: set the terminal title while
running (off by default), from a format string with the status
specifiers (default "rmut: %f").
.TP
.B status_on_top
mutt's, off by default: the status bar and message line sit near the
top, under the help bar, rather than at the bottom.
.TP
.B arrow_cursor
mutt's, off by default: mark the selected row with an arrow instead
of reverse video.
.TP
.B menu_scroll
When the cursor leaves the screen the index scrolls a line at a time
(on by default here, where mutt turns a page); off shows the next
page instead (mutt's $menu_scroll).
.TP
.B menu_context
Lines kept in view beyond the cursor when the index scrolls or pages
(mutt's $menu_context, 0).
.TP
.B menu_move_off
The last message may scroll up past the bottom of the screen (mutt's
$menu_move_off, on). Off keeps the index bottom\-stuck once it fills
the screen.
.TP
.B help
The key\-help bar on the top line (mutt's $help, on).
.TP
.B error_history
How many past errors error\-history shows (mutt's $error_history,
30). 0 disables it.
.TP
.B sort_browser
mutt's $sort_browser: the folder browser's order \(em alpha (the
default), count or unread (by new\-mail count), date (by the
maildir's change time), unsorted (as configured, then as found). A
reverse\- prefix flips it. size reads as alpha.
.TP
.B status_chars
mutt's $status_chars: the characters
.B %r
shows for the mailbox state \(em [0] unchanged, [1] changed, [2]
read\-only. Unset keeps rmut's own (nothing / * / %).
.SS [sidebar]
.TP
.B visible, width
The left mailbox pane (toggle at runtime with
.BR B ).
.SS [colors]
Slot overrides: status_fg, status_bg, deleted, flagged, header,
error (error statuses, mutt's `color error`, default bold bright
red). Quoted text tints by depth with quoted, quoted1, quoted2, ...
(default: cyan for every depth); pager search hits use search_fg /
search_bg (default: reverse video).
.SS [[color_index]]
Pattern rules for index lines:
.B pattern
plus
.B fg
and/or
.BR bg .
The first matching rule wins over the slot colors; broken rules warn
and drop. mutt's
.B "color index"
lines import here, except
.BR ~D ", " ~F " and " ~T
with no background, which have slots of their own
.RB ( deleted ", " flagged ", " tagged );
those slots hold one colour, so a line painting a background, like
.BR "color index black magenta \(dq~D\(dq" ,
keeps both as a rule instead.
.SS [[color_body]]
Regex rules for pager body spans (mutt's `color body`):
.B pattern
is a plain regex (not a message pattern) plus
.B fg
and/or
.BR bg ,
applied in order to every match on a displayed line \(em URLs, diff
lines, and the like.
.SS [filters]
MIME type \(-> shell command rendering the part (stdin to stdout),
e.g. "text/html" = "w3m \-dump \-T text/html" \(em mutt's auto_view.
Applied to matching parts wherever they sit in the message, preferred
in multipart/alternative, and used in the attachment viewer.
.PP
An empty command means mutt's arrangement: rmut takes it from mailcap
(RFC 1524), the first
.B copiousoutput
entry for the type, skipping one whose
.B test=
command fails or that asks for the terminal, and giving
.B %s
a temporary file holding the part. The files are $MAILCAPS when set,
otherwise ~/.mailcap, /etc/mailcap, /usr/etc/mailcap and
/usr/local/etc/mailcap. A type with no such entry does not autoview,
and the part shows as an attachment.
.SS [keys.index], [keys.pager]
Remaps,
.IR action " = " key ,
e.g. sync = "w". Action names appear in the
.B ?
help; keys are a single character,
.BR ctrl+x / alt+x ,
or a name: enter, esc, space, tab, backspace, up, down, pgup, pgdn,
home, end.
.SS [macros.index], [macros.pager]
.IR key " = " sequence
\(em the key replays the sequence (literal characters plus key names
in angle brackets: <enter>, <esc>, <ctrl+x>, ...). Macros feed the
input queue, so they can drive prompts, and shadow a binding on the
same key. For instance
.PP
.nf
  [macros.index]
  "." = "/~D<enter>"         # next message marked for deletion
  "," = "<alt+/>~D<enter>"   # and the previous one
.fi
.SS [[accounts]]
.TP
.B name, user
The account, opened as imap:name/FOLDER.
.TP
.B password_command
Shell command whose first stdout line is the password (pass(1)-style;
wins over
.BR password ).
.TP
.B password
Plaintext password \(em keep the file at mode 600.
.TP
.B imap_host, imap_port, imap_tls
IMAP server; port 993 with TLS from the first byte by default,
STARTTLS on any other port.
.TP
.B smtp_host, smtp_port, smtp_tls
SMTP submission; port 587 with STARTTLS by default, implicit TLS on
port 465.
.TP
.B auth, token_command
"password" (default), "xoauth2" or "oauthbearer"; the OAuth
mechanisms authenticate with a fresh access token from
.B token_command
(refresh is that tool's business \(em oauth2ms, mutt_oauth2.py).
.TP
.B sent_folder
IMAP folder receiving the Fcc copy of sent mail (default Sent).
.TP
.B identity
From identity when composing from this account's mailboxes, e.g.
identity = { name = "Jane Work", email = "jane@work.example.com" }.
.SS [pgp]
.TP
.B command
The gpg executable (default gpg). Decrypt/verify happens
automatically when a viewed message is PGP; signing and encrypting
are chosen in the compose menu (p). Passphrases are gpg-agent's business
\(em rmut never sees them.
.TP
.B sign_key
Signing key for \-\-local\-user; gpg's default key when unset.
.TP
.B sign_by_default, encrypt_by_default
Preselect signing/encrypting for new drafts.
.TP
.B reply_sign, reply_encrypt, reply_sign_encrypted
mutt's $crypt_replysign / $crypt_replyencrypt /
$crypt_replysignencrypted: a reply inherits the original's protection
\(em signed when it was signed, encrypted when it was encrypted, and
signed too when answering encrypted mail. Off by default; detection
reads the MIME type only, never decrypting.
.SH FORMAT STRINGS
.B index.format
takes
.BI % [\-][ min ][. max ] X
specifiers:
.B \-
left-aligns,
.I min
pads,
.I .max
truncates.
.B %C
number,
.B %Z
status/flag/mark characters (the mark is mutt's to_chars:
.B +
sole recipient,
.B T
one of several,
.B C
on the Cc,
.B F
sent by you,
.B L
to a subscribed list, and
.B *
for a tagged message),
.B %d
date,
.B %F
from,
.B %L
list-aware from ("To <list>" for List-Id mail),
.B %c
size,
.B %l
body lines,
.B %y
the X\-Label,
.B %M
messages hidden under a collapsed thread,
.B %s
subject,
.B %%
a literal percent. Mutt conditionals work:
.B %?X?then&else?
renders
.I then
when field X is set and non-zero. The default matches mutt's:
"%4C %Z %\-6d %\-15.15L (%?l?%4l&%4c?) %s".
.PP
.B ui.status_format
shares the syntax with these specifiers:
.B %f
mailbox,
.B %m
messages,
.B %M
shown-when-limited,
.B %n
new,
.B %u
unread,
.B %d
deleted,
.B %F
flagged,
.B %t
tagged,
.B %s
sort,
.B %V
limit pattern,
.B %r
mailbox mark (* pending changes, % read-only),
.B %P
index position (all/top/bot/NN%),
.B %v
version. A single
.B %>X
pushes everything after it to the right edge, filling the gap with
X, so mutt's default status line renders as-is. The default
reproduces the classic line:
"\-\-\-rmut%r: %f [Msgs:%?M?%M/?%m New:%n%?d? Del:%d?] (sort:%s)%?V? (limit:%V)?".
.SH MUTTRC IMPORT
.B rmut \-\-import\-muttrc
prints a translated configuration to stdout, or with
.B \-w
writes it straight to the config file, creating the directory and
refusing to overwrite one that is already there:
.PP
.nf
  rmut \-\-import\-muttrc \-w
  rmut \-\-import\-muttrc > ~/.config/rmut/config.toml
.fi
.PP
Alias lines, including ones in a
.B source
d file, go to the alias file rather than the config: rmut keeps them
in mutt's own format, so an existing alias file works as it stands.
With
.B \-w
they are written to ~/.config/rmut/aliases (or $RMUT_ALIASES); without
it they are printed as a comment block naming that file. A muttrc that
sets
.B alias_file
keeps it: that path is imported as [mail] alias_file, rmut reads and
appends to it, and the import writes no alias file of its own.
.PP
It understands identities, mailboxes, sendmail, editor, IMAP/SMTP
accounts (including imap_authenticators/smtp_authenticators for
OAuth), aliases, bindings, macros made of plain keys, color index
rules, index_format, status_format, trash and more. Anything without
an rmut equivalent is kept as a comment for review; stored passwords
next to an OAuth setup are redacted.
.SH CREDENTIALS
An account's password can sit in the config as
.BR password ,
which is where
.B \-\-import\-muttrc
puts
.BR "set imap_pass" ,
and it is the weakest of the options: the config is a plain file and
whoever reads it reads your mail. rmut creates the files it writes at
mode 600 and says so at startup when a config holding a password is
readable by anyone else.
.PP
.B password_command
keeps the secret elsewhere: its first line of output is the password,
it runs once per session, and a failure takes the connection down
with its message, so a locked store fails closed. Anything that
prints the password will do:
.PP
.nf
  password_command = "pass show mail/work"
  password_command = "gpg -q -d ~/.config/rmut/imap.gpg"
  password_command = "secret-tool lookup service imap user jane"
  password_command = "cat /media/crypt/mail-pass"
.fi
.PP
For OAuth2,
.B auth
= xoauth2 or oauthbearer with
.B token_command
runs the helper for every connection, tokens being short-lived.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/rmut/config.toml
The configuration
.RB ( $RMUT_CONFIG
overrides).
.TP
.I ~/.config/rmut/aliases
Mutt-style alias file, lines of "alias nick address..."
.RB ( [mail] alias_file
then
.B $RMUT_ALIASES
override).
.TP
.I ~/.mailcap
Where an auto_view type with an empty [filters] command gets one
.RB ( $MAILCAPS
overrides the search path; /etc/mailcap and the rest follow).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/rmut/
IMAP caches, mbox mirrors and the header cache
.RB ( $XDG_CACHE_HOME
overrides the parent).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RMUT_CONFIG, RMUT_ALIASES
Alternate configuration and alias files.
.TP
.B MAILCAPS
Colon-separated mailcap files, searched in order for an auto_view
command.
.TP
.B MAIL
Fallback mailbox when none is configured.
.TP
.B VISUAL, EDITOR
Draft editor when
.B mail.editor
is unset.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mutt (1),
.BR sendmail (8),
.BR gpg (1),
